@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In Pascal, variable names use @code{case-insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{CamelCase} or @code{snake_case}).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in lowercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: and, array, begin, case, const, destructor, division, do, downto, else, end, file, for, function, goto, if, implementation, in, label, mod, nil, not, object, of, or, packed, procedure, program, record, repeat, set, shl, shr, string, then, to, type, unit, until, uses, var, while, with, xor
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, div, mod, =, <>, <, >, <=, >=, and, or, not, <<, >>
@item String delimiters: Single quotes (@code{'...'}) or double quotes (@code{"..."}) for string literals
@item Comments: Curly braces (@code{@{ ... @}) or (* ... *) or // to end of line
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A Pascal program consists of a program heading, optional uses clause, block of declarations and statements.

@example
program GameLogic;

uses
  SysUtils;

var
  Score: Integer = 0;
  LivesLeft: Integer = 3;
  CurrentRoom: Integer = 1;

procedure InitializeGame;
begin
  Score := 0;
  LivesLeft := 3;
  CurrentRoom := 1;
end;

procedure ProcessInput;
var
  Input: string;
begin
  ReadLn(Input);
  if Input = 'MOVE NORTH' then
    CurrentRoom := CurrentRoom + 10;
end;

procedure UpdateGame;
begin
  // Game logic updates
end;

procedure RenderDisplay;
begin
  // Rendering code
end;

begin
  InitializeGame;
  while LivesLeft > 0 do
  begin
    ProcessInput;
    UpdateGame;
    RenderDisplay;
  end;
end.
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

Pascal supports object-oriented programming through classes and methods.

@example
type
  TPlayer = class
  private
    FX: Integer;
    FY: Integer;
    FSprite: Integer;
  public
    constructor Create(X, Y, Sprite: Integer);
    procedure Move(DX, DY: Integer);
    property X: Integer read FX write FX;
    property Y: Integer read FY write FY;
    property Sprite: Integer read FSprite write FSprite;
  end;

constructor TPlayer.Create(X, Y, Sprite: Integer);
begin
  inherited Create;
  FX := X;
  FY := Y;
  FSprite := Sprite;
end;

procedure TPlayer.Move(DX, DY: Integer);
begin
  FX := FX + DX;
  FY := FY + DY;
end;

var
  Player: TPlayer;

begin
  Player := TPlayer.Create(100, 100, 5);
  // Use player
end.
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a procedure or function that takes no arguments:

@example
InitializeGraphics;
ClearScreen;
PlayStartupMusic;
SomeProcedure;
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a function that returns a single value:

@example
Score := CalculateBonus(Level, TimeLeft);
BonusFactor := GetInputFactor;
Volume := GetMasterVolume;
SomeFunction;
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in Pascal's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a procedure with parameters:

@example
DrawSprite(SpriteId, XPos, YPos);
PlaySound(SoundId, Volume, LoopFlag);
CheckCollision(Obj1, Obj2, ResultVar);
SomeProcedure(Param1, Param2);
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

Pascal accesses library routines through standard procedure/function calls or object methods:

@example
LoadLevel(LevelNumber);          @r{Direct call}
FarProc(LibraryAddress);         @r{Far call}
Result := LibraryFunction(Input); @r{Library function}
Object.Method(Param1, Param2);   @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: ProcedureName(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Far: AbsoluteAddress(...) (via external unit)
@item Library: LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: OBJECT.MethodName(...)
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in Pascal are called using external units or the absolute directive:

@example
// Declare far procedure
procedure FarLoadLibrary(Addr: LongWord); external 'farproc' name 'FarLoadLibrary';

// Call far routine
FarLoadLibrary($8000);   @r{Call routine in bank 1}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: External procedure call
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in Pascal uses dot notation:

@example
Player.Move(5, 10);
ScoreDisplay.Update(Score);
GameOver := GameState.IsGameOver;
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via property assignment
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: METHOD invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using the assignment operator (:=):

@example
Score := 0;                           @r{Explicit assignment}
LivesLeft := 3;                       @r{Implicit assignment}
Counter := Counter + 1;               @r{Increment}
LivesLeft := LivesLeft - 1;           @r{Decrement}
Score := Score + 10;                  @r{Arithmetic assignment}
Message := 'Hello, World!';           @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (properties) are assigned using property assignment:

@example
Player.X := 160;                      @r{Set object's x coordinate}
Player.Y := 100;                      @r{Set object's y coordinate}
Player.Sprite := 5;                   @r{Set sprite number}
Player.Visible := True;               @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

Array elements are assigned using indexing:

@example
var
  ScoreList: array[0..9] of Integer;
  InitialsList: array[0..9] of string;
  Board: array[0..7, 0..7] of Integer;

begin
  ScoreList[0] := 100;
  InitialsList[0] := 'ALI';
  Board[2][3] := 1;
end.
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - operators:

@example
Score := Score + 10;                  @r{Addition}
LivesLeft := LivesLeft - 1;           @r{Subtraction}
Total := Width + Height;              @r{Add two variables}
FinalPrice := Price - Discount;       @r{Subtraction}
Delta := NewValue - OldValue;         @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two uses the shl and shr operators:

@example
Result := Value shl 2;                @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
Result := Value shr 1;                @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
Result := Value * 4;                  @r{Multiply by 4}
Result := Value div 2;                @r{Divide by 2}
Result := Value * 8;                  @r{Multiply by 8}
Result := Value div 16;               @r{Divide by 16}
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
Total := (BaseSalary * 12) + Bonus - Deductions;
Average := (Sum1 + Sum2 + Sum3) / 3.0;
Distance := Sqrt(Sqr(X2 - X1) + Sqr(Y2 - Y1));
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and comparison operators:

@example
if Score > 1000 then ...              @r{Greater than}
if Score < 50 then ...                @r{Less than}
if Score = 100 then ...               @r{Equality}
if Score <> 0 then ...                @r{Inequality}
if (X >= 0) and (X < MAX_X) then ...  @r{Combined conditions}
if (Button = BUTTON_A) or (Button = BUTTON_B) then ... @r{Alternative conditions}
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses if/then/else/end blocks:

@example
if Temperature > THOT then
  Status := 'HOT'
else if Temperature > TCOLD then
  Status := 'WARM'
else
  Status := 'COLD';

case Answer of
  'Y', 'y': Response := 'YES';
  'N', 'n': Response := 'NO';
  else    Response := 'INVALID';
end;
@end example

Pascal does not have an EXAMINE/COND structure; case statement serves
a similar purpose for multi-way branching.

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using for, while, and repeat-until loops:

@example
// for loop equivalent (known iterations)
for I := 1 to 10 do
  Writeln('Count: ', I);

// while loop equivalent (pre-test)
I := 1;
while I <= 10 do
begin
  Writeln('Count: ', I);
  Inc(I);
end;

// repeat-until loop equivalent (post-test)
repeat
  Write('Continue? (Y/N): ');
  ReadLn(Response);
until UpCase(Response) = 'Y';

// for loop with break and continue
for I := 1 to 100 do
begin
  if Array[I] = TARGET_VALUE then
  begin
    Writeln('Found at index: ', I);
    Break;                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
  end;
  if Array[I] < 0 then
    Continue;                 @r{Skip to next iteration}
  // Process positive values
end;
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using Break and Continue statements:

@example
Exit for loop early
for I := 1 to 100 do
begin
  if Array[I] = TARGET_VALUE then
  begin
    Writeln('Found at index: ', I);
    Break;                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
  end;
end;

// Skip current iteration
for I := 1 to 100 do
begin
  if Array[I] < 0 then
    Continue;                 @r{Skip to next iteration}
  // Process non-negative values
end;

// Nested loop control
for I := 1 to 10 do
begin
  for J := 1 to 10 do
  begin
    if Matrix[I][J] = 0 then
      Break;                @r{Exit inner loop only}
    // Process non-zero elements
  end;
end;
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

Pascal does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own procedures. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, Pascal commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
// Original Pascal code
if (X > 100) and (X < 200) then
  ShowMessage(MSG_WELCOME);

// Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
if ((X > 100) and (X < 200)) then
  (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil);
end;
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
// Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
// Assuming direct memory access via pointer
var
  VideoMemory: Pointer;
  Offset: Integer;
begin
  VideoMemory := Ptr($B8000);
  Offset := (Row * 80 + Col) * 2;
  PByteArray(VideoMemory)^[Offset] := TileNum;
  PByteArray(VideoMemory)^[Offset + 1] := Attribute;
end;

// Library routine approach
LoadTileSet(TileSetId);
PlaceTile(Layer, X, Y, TileIndex);
ScrollBackground(DX, DY);
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
// Hardware sprite approach (if available)
InitSprites;
SetSpritePosition(SpriteId, X, Y);
SetSpriteImage(SpriteId, ImageId);
ShowSprite(SpriteId, -1);   @r{-1 = visible}

// Software sprite approach
type
  TSprite = record
    X, Y, Frame: Integer;
  end;

var
  Sprite: TSprite;

begin
  Sprite.X := 100;
  Sprite.Y := 50;
  Sprite.Frame := 0;

  Sprite.X := 100;
  Sprite.Y := 50;
  Sprite.Frame := (Sprite.Frame + 1) mod 4;
end.
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
// Using classes
type
  TGameObject = class
  private
    FX, FY: Integer;
    FWidth, FHeight: Integer;
    FActive: Boolean;
  public
    constructor Create(X, Y, Width, Height: Integer);
    procedure Move(DX, DY: Integer);
    property X: Integer read FX write FX;
    property Y: Integer read FY write FY;
    property Width: Integer read FWidth write FWidth;
    property Height: Integer read FHeight write FHeight;
    property Active: Boolean read FActive write FActive;
  end;

constructor TGameObject.Create(X, Y, Width, Height: Integer);
begin
  inherited Create;
  FX := X;
  FY := Y;
  FWidth := Width;
  FHeight := Height;
  FActive := True;
end;

procedure TGameObject.Move(DX, DY: Integer);
begin
  FX := FX + DX;
  FY := FY + DY;
end;

var
  GameObjects: array[0..19] of TGameObject;
  I: Integer;

begin
  for I := 0 to 19 do
    GameObjects[I] := TGameObject.Create(100, 100, 16, 16);
  for I := 0 to 19 do
  begin
    GameObjects[I].Move(DX, DY);
    if GameObjects[I].Active then
      DrawObject(GameObjects[I]);
  end;
end.
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following Pascal features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (assign, reset, rewrite, close, read, write)
@item Graphics commands (Graph unit: line, circle, floodfill, etc.)
@item Hardware-specific features (joystick, mouse, keyboard beyond basic)
@item Extended memory handling (unless emulated)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution
@item Exception handling (try, except, finally, raise)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives ($define, $ifdef)
@item Object-oriented multiple inheritance (unless using interfaces)
@item Generics and anonymous methods (unless using modern Pascal)
@end table

